# A t3.micro has only 1GB RAM and no swap by default - a transient spike (a slow request holding
# a few too many connections open, a burst of concurrent uploads) has nowhere to go but straight
# to the OOM killer, which is exactly the kind of thing that left the EB health agent unresponsive
# during the on-instance Maven build (see Dockerfile.deploy's own history/comment - that specific
# cause is now fixed by building off-instance, but this is a general safety net, not a fix for
# that one incident). A swap file turns "OOM kill" into "slower, but survives" for a transient
# spike - it's not a substitute for the -Xmx cap in Dockerfile.deploy, the two work together (heap
# capped so the JVM itself behaves predictably, swap as the last-resort net underneath everything
# else on the box: OS, Docker daemon, EB/SSM agents).
#
# `commands:` runs on the host instance itself, before the app container starts - this is the
# standard mechanism for Beanstalk's single-container Docker platform (.platform/hooks is the
# alternative on some platform versions, but .ebextensions commands: works uniformly here and
# needs no separate script file). The `test:` guard makes this idempotent across redeploys to the
# same instance - without it, a redeploy would try to re-fallocate an already-swapped-on file.
commands:
  01_create_swapfile:
    test: "[ ! -f /swapfile ]"
    command: |
      fallocate -l 512M /swapfile
      chmod 600 /swapfile
      mkswap /swapfile
      swapon /swapfile
      echo '/swapfile none swap sw 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
